{"name":"Watusi","hex":"#f2cdbb","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Watusi","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A member of a Bantu-speaking people living in Rwanda and Burundi; the Tutsi.","A cow of a modern American breed."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Watusi"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tutsi","blocks":[{"text":"The Tutsi, also called Watusi, Watutsi or Abatutsi, are an ethnic group established primarily in Rwanda and Burundi. They are a Bantu-speaking people and the second-largest of three main ethnic groups in Rwanda and Burundi, the other two being the Hutu and Twa."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tutsi"},{"title":"Watusi (album)","blocks":[{"text":"Watusi is a studio album by The Wedding Present. It was released in 1994 on Island Records. It peaked at No. 47 on the UK Albums chart."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/a/a2/Watusi_%28album%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Watusi_(album)"},{"title":"Watusi (dance)","blocks":[{"text":"The Watusi is a solo dance that enjoyed brief popularity during the early 1960s. It was one of the most popular dance crazes of the 1960s in the United States. \"Watusi\" is a former name for the Tutsi people of Africa, whose traditions include spectacular dances. The naming of the American dance may have been inspired, in particular, by a scene in the 1950 film King Solomon's Mines which featured Tutsi dancers, or by its sequel Watusi."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/e/e4/Watusi_%28dance%29.jpg/330px-Watusi_%28dance%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Watusi_(dance)"},{"title":"Watusi (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Watusi is a 1959 American adventure film, starring George Montgomery, Taina Elg, and David Farrar. Directed by Kurt Neumann, the film is a sequel to the 1950 film King Solomon's Mines. It was produced by Al Zimbalist and Donald Zimbalist. The screenplay was by James Clavell loosely based on the 1885 novel King Solomon's Mines by H. Rider Haggard."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/d/d2/Watusi_%28film%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Watusi_(film)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/King%27s_palace_in_Nyanza.jpg/500px-King%27s_palace_in_Nyanza.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/King%27s_palace_in_Nyanza.jpg/500px-King%27s_palace_in_Nyanza.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKing's_palace_in_Nyanza.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Chifu_Kaware_safarini_%28Kandt_1904_II%2C_97%29.png/500px-Chifu_Kaware_safarini_%28Kandt_1904_II%2C_97%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Chifu_Kaware_safarini_%28Kandt_1904_II%2C_97%29.png/500px-Chifu_Kaware_safarini_%28Kandt_1904_II%2C_97%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChifu_Kaware_safarini_(Kandt_1904_II%2C_97).png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Brooklyn_Museum_22.1403_Wrist_Guard_Igitembe.jpg/500px-Brooklyn_Museum_22.1403_Wrist_Guard_Igitembe.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Brooklyn_Museum_22.1403_Wrist_Guard_Igitembe.jpg/1280px-Brooklyn_Museum_22.1403_Wrist_Guard_Igitembe.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https: